# 7. Automate publishing with GitHub Actions

The project uses this workflow file:

```text
.github/workflows/ci.yml
```

Its purpose is to build and publish the Docker image automatically whenever code is pushed to `main`, or when the workflow is manually started.

## 7.1 Final workflow configuration

The workflow now contains two image tags:

```yaml
name: Docker Build and Push

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main
  workflow_dispatch:

jobs:
  build-and-push:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Docker Buildx
        uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3

      - name: Log in to Docker Hub
        uses: docker/login-action@v3
        with:
          username: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_USERNAME }}
          password: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_TOKEN }}

      - name: Build and push Docker image
        uses: docker/build-push-action@v6
        with:
          context: .
          push: true
          tags: |
            kelyunice1419/container-deployment-practice:1.0
            kelyunice1419/container-deployment-practice:${{ github.sha }}
```

## 7.2 Understand the workflow

### Trigger

```yaml
on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main
  workflow_dispatch:
```

This means the workflow can run after a push to `main` or through a manual workflow dispatch.

### Runner

```yaml
runs-on: ubuntu-latest
```

GitHub provides a hosted Ubuntu runner to execute the workflow.

### Checkout

```yaml
uses: actions/checkout@v4
```

Downloads the repository contents into the runner's workspace.

### Docker Buildx

```yaml
uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3
```

Prepares Docker Buildx for building the image.

### Authentication

```yaml
username: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_USERNAME }}
password: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_TOKEN }}
```

Reads the Docker Hub credentials from GitHub repository secrets.

### Build and push

```yaml
context: .
push: true
```

Uses the repository root as the build context and pushes the built image to Docker Hub.

The `tags` block gives the built image two tags.

