# 10. Introduce commit-SHA image tagging

Initially, the workflow published only:

```yaml
tags: kelyunice1419/container-deployment-practice:1.0
```

This tag can be updated by later builds. Consequently, it does not permanently identify a particular build.

We changed the workflow to publish both:

```yaml
tags: |
  kelyunice1419/container-deployment-practice:1.0
  kelyunice1419/container-deployment-practice:${{ github.sha }}
```

## What is `github.sha`?

`github.sha` is a GitHub Actions context value containing the full commit SHA for the commit associated with the workflow run.

A commit SHA provides a way to identify the source revision associated with that image build.

For example, the published image tag we observed was:

```text
1774f0616839382e12bdf611f521e8da23317c56
```

This was the full SHA associated with the workflow update commit.

Docker Hub link:

https://hub.docker.com/repository/docker/kelyunice1419/container-deployment-practice/tags/1774f0616839382e12bdf611f521e8da23317c56

## 10.1 Verify the workflow file

```bash
