# 15. Lessons learned

This project taught and reinforced several core DevOps concepts.

### Version control

Git tracks changes, while commits provide identifiable snapshots of the repository. `git add`, `git commit`, and `git push` serve different purposes.

### Containerization

A Dockerfile defines how an image is built. A container is a running instance created from an image.

### Image tags

A tag is a human-readable reference to an image. The `1.0` tag is used as a moving reference in this workflow, while the commit-SHA tag identifies the source revision associated with a build.

### Registry publishing

Docker Hub stores images so they can be retrieved on another machine or after a local image has been removed.

### CI/CD

GitHub Actions automates the image build and publishing process after a push to the configured branch.

### Secrets management

Authentication credentials belong in secret storage, not in source code or public repositories.

### Rollback

A rollback depends on having a known-good image available and knowing how to retrieve and run it. Testing the old image on a separate port allowed us to validate it without interrupting the existing applications.

